&lt;p&gt;A political campaign CRM is a system for managing supporter relationships and coordinating campaign interactions across teams. Unlike a general CRM, a political campaign CRM focuses on the needs of election campaigns, including supporter engagement, volunteer coordination, donor relationships, and outreach.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=b173b371-487a-4b24-8d8d-508e4cff3779&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Learn more about why HubSpot's CRM platform has all the tools you need to grow  better." height="58" width="802" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/b173b371-487a-4b24-8d8d-508e4cff3779.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A campaign CRM works alongside other systems rather than replacing them. Campaign teams use a dedicated voter-file system for voter data and specialized platforms for fundraising and compliance, while the CRM manages the campaign’s own relationships and engagement. This setup helps teams connect contact management, outreach, and follow-up across the election cycle.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This guide covers the core capabilities political campaigns should evaluate, the boundaries between a CRM and specialized political systems, and how general-purpose CRM platforms can fit into a campaign technology stack. It also looks at data maintenance, permissions, consent, communication workflows, and data retention between election cycles.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-is-a-crm-for-political-campaigns"&gt;What is a CRM for political campaigns?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-a-political-crm-differs-from-a-business-crm"&gt;How a Political CRM Differs From a Business CRM&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#crm-for-political-campaigns-features-to-prioritize"&gt;CRM for Political Campaigns Features to Prioritize&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-choose-a-campaign-management-crm"&gt;How to Choose a Campaign Management CRM&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#where-hubspot-fits-for-political-campaign-software"&gt;Where HubSpot Fits for Political Campaign Software&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#political-crm-and-campaign-management-glossary"&gt;Political CRM and Campaign Management Glossary&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#frequently-asked-questions-about-political-crms"&gt;Frequently Asked Questions About Political CRMs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#using-a-crm-for-political-campaigns-where-it-fits-best"&gt;Using a CRM for Political Campaigns Where It Fits Best&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What is a CRM for political campaigns?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A political campaign CRM centralizes voter, donor, volunteer, and outreach data. It gives campaign teams a single place to organize supporter records and connect them to outreach workflows. A CRM can also give campaign staff a shared view of supporter activity, such as past interactions, communication preferences, volunteer involvement, or donation history.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;How a Political CRM Differs From a Business CRM&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A political campaign CRM differs from a business CRM through voter-file, district, precinct, volunteer, and compliance workflows. While a business CRM typically organizes customer and sales relationships, political campaign software must account for election-specific data and operations such as:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Geographic districts&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Volunteer activity&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Supporter relationships&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Campaign communications&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These differences also affect data management. Campaign teams may need to connect voter-file data with supporter records, volunteer information, outreach history, and other campaign systems.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In the U.S., voter-file access and permitted uses can vary by jurisdiction, while campaign-finance requirements add another layer of compliance. General-purpose CRMs may therefore require &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/sales/crm-integration"&gt;dedicated integrations&lt;/a&gt; or separate systems for U.S. campaign-finance and FEC reporting.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;U.S. campaign finance reporting and FEC filing typically run through dedicated systems rather than a general CRM.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;CRM for Political Campaigns Features to Prioritize&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The best political campaign tools should make it easier to organize supporter relationships, coordinate volunteers, communicate with contacts, and understand outreach performance without replacing specialized election systems. When evaluating a CRM for a campaign, look for the following features, but treat them as capabilities to evaluate rather than as features that every general CRM includes natively.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://blog.hubspot.com/hubfs/blogbot/crm-for-political-campaigns-1-20261116-3951995.webp" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;" alt="crm for political campaigns features to prioritize"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Voter Contact Management&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A campaign CRM should give teams a structured way to manage their own supporter contact records, including contact details and interaction history. Those records can then support audience segmentation based on campaign-defined criteria, helping teams organize supporters by:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Geography&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Engagement&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Interests&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Previous interactions&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;It’s important to note that supporter contact records are not the voter file. A general CRM should not be treated as the authoritative source for voter registration status, voting history, or other voter-file information. The dedicated voter-file system remains the source for that data, while the CRM holds the campaign’s own relationship and engagement records.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Supporter records can change quickly during an election cycle, so &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/what-is-crm-data-maintenance"&gt;ongoing CRM data maintenance&lt;/a&gt; is essential. Campaign teams should regularly check contact details, duplicate records, engagement status, consent, and segmentation criteria to keep supporter data accurate and prevent outdated records from driving future outreach.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Volunteer Coordination&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A campaign CRM can help organize the process of turning volunteer interest into active participation. Instead of keeping volunteer information in separate spreadsheets, forms, or inboxes, teams can capture contact details and interests in one place.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A volunteer can submit a form that creates or updates a contact record, triggers an automated confirmation, and creates a task for a campaign staff member to follow up. Teams can then use volunteer records, assignments, and engagement history to keep track of who has been contacted, who still needs follow-up, and which volunteers are ready for the next step.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Donor Relationship Tracking&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Managing donor relationships requires a clear view of where each relationship stands and what should happen next. A CRM provides campaign staff with a structured way to manage ongoing donor engagement, while a donor pipeline can help track prospective and existing donors...

&lt;p&gt;For SEO managers, volatility is nothing new (Google has been hitting us with algorithm update surprises for years). But AI search volatility adds a new layer of complexity. Plenty of teams have a stake in those swings: In HubSpot’s &lt;a href="https://offers.hubspot.com/state-of-aeo"&gt;State of AEO report&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;58% of marketers said their organizations were optimizing content specifically for answer engines&lt;/strong&gt; in Q1 2026.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When a Google core update hits, you can at least open your rank tracker and see where your pages landed. But AI answers don’t come with a stable position to check. And a single model update can change how many businesses ChatGPT, Gemini, or Perplexity link to in their answers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=07ca2318-c9e0-4b5d-bcba-95cec5cb4958&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Download Now: The State of AEO in 2026 [Free AI Search Trends Report]" height="79" width="369" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/07ca2318-c9e0-4b5d-bcba-95cec5cb4958.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So when your AI visibility or AI-referred traffic drops, your boss will want to know: Was it something we did, or the answer engines? &lt;strong&gt;To answer that, compare your brand’s AEO data with a &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-sensor"&gt;market-wide benchmark&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt; for the same time period.&lt;/strong&gt; If the industry benchmark dropped alongside yours, you’re likely looking at a market-wide shift, not a flaw in your strategy. If the benchmark held steady, the problem probably sits closer to home: your content, your site, or your tracking setup. Below, I’ll show you how to measure AI search volatility, diagnose issues, and report the swings to leadership without causing a panic.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-is-ai-search-volatility"&gt;What is AI search volatility?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-ai-search-volatility-differs-from-ranking-volatility"&gt;How AI Search Volatility Differs From Ranking Volatility&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-measure-ai-search-volatility"&gt;How to Measure AI Search Volatility&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-tell-if-ai-search-volatility-is-market-wide"&gt;How to Tell If AI Search Volatility Is Market-Wide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-compare-ai-search-benchmarks-with-your-aeo-data"&gt;How to Compare AI Search Benchmarks With Your AEO Data&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-report-ai-search-volatility-to-leadership"&gt;How to Report AI Search Volatility to Leadership&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-to-do-when-ai-search-volatility-spikes"&gt;What to Do When AI Search Volatility Spikes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#frequently-asked-questions-about-ai-search-volatility"&gt;Frequently Asked Questions About AI Search Volatility&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What is AI search volatility?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI search volatility refers to the fluctuation in which brands, pages, and sources answer engines like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Perplexity mention and cite from one day (or one prompt) to the next. General search instability is broader: Google ranking updates, seasonal demand swings, anything that changes how people find your website in the SERPs. AI search volatility focuses on which brands are mentioned in AI answers rather than where those brands’ webpages rank in search results.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Some of that fluctuation is built into the way LLMs work; they’re non-deterministic, meaning they don’t return an identical answer each turn. When &lt;a href="https://sparktoro.com/blog/new-research-ais-are-highly-inconsistent-when-recommending-brands-or-products-marketers-should-take-care-when-tracking-ai-visibility/"&gt;SparkToro and Gumshoe.ai&lt;/a&gt; ran the same recommendation prompts repeatedly in late 2025, ChatGPT and Google’s AI Overviews/AI Mode had less than a 1-in-100 chance of returning the same brand list twice. Because that randomness lives within the answer engines, a visibility dip doesn’t automatically mean your content got worse.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So how do you tell everyday noise from a real shift? You need broader market context. HubSpot’s free &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-sensor"&gt;AI Search Sensor&lt;/a&gt; dashboard includes an Answer Engine Volatility Tracker. It gives you a daily score showing how far four signals (mention rate, citation rate, citation type, and AI-referred traffic) have drifted from their rolling averages across ChatGPT, Gemini, and Perplexity. That way, you can tell if a spike or drop in your AEO metrics is affecting just you or your competitors too.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pro tip:&lt;/strong&gt; A new daily score may still adjust slightly over its first three days, so give it time to settle before you react.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;How AI Search Volatility Differs From Ranking Volatility&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In SEO, ranking volatility describes how much pages move up and down in Google’s search rankings. In AEO, AI search volatility describes how much answer engines change which brands they name and cite.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A SERP volatility tracker like Semrush Sensor scores Google’s day-to-day ranking movement on a 0-10 scale from a fixed keyword sample. That score can’t show what AI answers say about your brand, though.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Ranking volatility and AI search volatility can still overlap. In HubSpot’s &lt;a href="https://offers.hubspot.com/state-of-aeo"&gt;2026 State of AEO report&lt;/a&gt;, pages that ranked for more Google search terms, and ranked higher, were more likely to be cited by answer engines (a correlation, not proof that rankings &lt;em&gt;cause&lt;/em&gt; citations). High-ranking pages were also more likely to be cited in Google’s AI Overviews than in other answer engines. So, a Google ranking drop may show up as an AI Overviews citation drop too, which is why a SERP volatility tracker is still worth checking next to AI Search Sensor.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Pages with AI Overviews also tend to get fewer clicks. In &lt;a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/07/22/google-users-are-less-likely-to-click-on-links-when-an-ai-summary-appears-in-the-results/"&gt;Pew Research Center’s analysis&lt;/a&gt; of March 2025 browsing data from 900 U.S. adults, Google pages with an AI summary led to a traditional-result click in 8% of visits, versus 15% without an AI summary. So, a page can hold its rank and still lose traffic.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;That’s why &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/your-google-rank-doesnt-matter-anymore"&gt;rank-based KPIs&lt;/a&gt; alone can’t diagnose AEO performance. Search Console &lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features"&gt;folds AI Overviews and AI Mode&lt;/a&gt; into your overall traffic. Its newer &lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2026/06/gen-ai-performance-reports"&gt;generative AI reports&lt;/a&gt; break out AI-feature impressions, but only for Google.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;As MarketingProfs CCO Ann Handley noted in a &lt;a href="https://clutch.co/resources/state-of-content-marketing-ai"&gt;Clutch report&lt;/a&gt;, traffic helps gauge reach, “but it’s misleading when it stands alone for success.”&lt;/p&gt; 
&...

&lt;p&gt;If you’ve yet to understand Claude Cowork skills, let me help you out: Claude Cowork is a mode that can access your desktop files and complete tasks for you. A skill is a set of instructions that tell Claude exactly how to perform a specific workflow. When combined, the two can boost your productivity in a way that Claude Chat alone cannot.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/glossary/claude-skills"&gt;Claude skills&lt;/a&gt; aren’t limited to one Claude surface or tool. Skills work in Claude Chat, Claude Code, &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; Claude Cowork. So “Claude Cowork skills” are actually a feature across the AI, not just a single mode.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=64493837-f4dd-43b0-abee-3ef154e06092&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Connect HubSpot to Claude For Personalized Insights [Free Connector]" height="58" width="645" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/64493837-f4dd-43b0-abee-3ef154e06092.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This is even truer since &lt;a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/16/anthropic-merges-claude-chat-and-cowork-in-one-interface/"&gt;Anthropic announced&lt;/a&gt; that Claude Cowork, Chat, Artifacts, and Design are merging into a single unified experience, starting in October 2026. Skills, projects, connectors, and tasks all carry over, but users no longer have to dance between tabs. Claude simply decides which tools to use based on what you ask.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These changes are meant to reduce confusion, but it’s natural to still have questions. We’ll clear things up as they stand now, share what Claude Cowork skills are, and what you should build to get the most out of them.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Note: These changes are rolling out to Claude’s Pro and Max plans first, on the web, desktop, and mobile over the coming weeks, with plans to extend them to the free and team tiers later.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-are-claude-cowork-skills"&gt;What are Claude Cowork skills?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#vs"&gt;Claude Skills vs. Projects vs. Instructions vs. Memory&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-claude"&gt;How Claude Cowork Skills Work&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-create"&gt;How to Create Your First Claude Cowork Skill&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#best-uses"&gt;Claude Cowork Skills: Best Uses for Revenue Teams&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#best-practices"&gt;Claude Cowork Best Practices for Safety and Reliability&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#connect"&gt;Connect Claude to HubSpot&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#faq"&gt;Frequently Asked Questions About Claude Cowork Skills&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#all"&gt;All it takes is one &lt;span style="font-style: italic;"&gt;skill&lt;/span&gt; to start.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What are Claude Cowork skills?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A Claude Cowork Skill is a reusable instruction folder (with optional supporting templates, scripts, or reference documents attached) that Claude loads when a task needs it, and ignores when it doesn’t. You can use Claude Cowork skills across every other Claude surface, so truly, any Claude skill is a Claude Cowork skill and vice versa.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;If you want to get into the nuts and bolts of it, a Claude skill is technically a folder that can contain the following:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructions:&lt;/strong&gt; the step-by-step procedure and the rules Claude should follow.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Templates and reference files:&lt;/strong&gt; brand guidelines, sample outputs, or layouts that show the standard to match.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scripts:&lt;/strong&gt; code for the deterministic parts, such as reformatting data or generating a file.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;What makes a skill different from a detailed prompt you paste every time is:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It’s portable.&lt;/strong&gt; One skill works in Chat, Cowork, and Claude Code; you don’t rewrite it for each surface. You can also use Claude skills in &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/which-llm-should-you-use"&gt;different AI or LLM tools&lt;/a&gt; like ChatGPT.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It’s consistent.&lt;/strong&gt; Claude follows the same steps, in the same order, every time. No drift, no improvising.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It’s smart about context.&lt;/strong&gt; Claude only loads what it needs, which keeps the conversation focused and efficient.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Thanks to Cowork and the recent merger, a skill can now also access internal documentation and do things like reading and writing to folders on your computer, running multi-step tasks autonomously, coordinating &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/how-to-build-ai-agents"&gt;sub-agents&lt;/a&gt;, and producing finished files like spreadsheets and presentations, not just responding in text like it would with Chat alone.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When you need Claude to repeat a multi-step process the same way every time (i.e., formatting a weekly report, triaging leads, summarizing support tickets), that’s where a skill comes in handy.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For example, when I’m using Claude for fact-checking assistance, I often prompt it with detailed instructions on what to verify in the article, how to verify it, and what to include in its citations so that I can double-check its work. Instead of repeating that prompt every single time I’m done with an article, I have a “blog-fact-checker” skill that contains my detailed instructions. Now, instead of 500 words, I simply type two words, “Fact check,” and attach my draft via Google Docs. Easy.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Note: Record a Skill is a Cowork feature still exclusive to Claude Cowork on Mac desktops with Pro, Max, and Team plans, but we’ll get into that later.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Where to Access Claude Skills&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Claude automatically selects and uses skills depending on the context. You don’t have to tell it when to use a skill or which one to invoke, though you &lt;em&gt;can&lt;/em&gt; do so in a few ways:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;1. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;When creating a skill, you can include explicit wording in the skill description on how Claude can know when to invoke the skill (&lt;a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices%23:~:text%3DAlways%2520write%2520in%2520third%2520person.%2520The%2520description%2520is%2520injected%2520into%2520the%2520system%2520prompt%252C%2520and%2520inconsistent%2520point%252Dof%252Dview%2520can%2520cause%2520discovery%2520problems."&gt;Anthropic recommends writing in third person&lt;/a&gt;). Example: “Use this skill whenever the user says ‘generate an image,’ ‘make an infographic,’ or ‘create an image.’”&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;2. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Add the instructions to your prompt. Example: “Use your infographic skill to create a bar chart showing the monthly sales comparison.”&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;3. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Click the 
&lt;strong&gt;+ &lt;/strong&gt;icon to the lower left of the chat, hover over 
&lt;strong&gt;Skills&lt;/strong&gt;, and select the skill you want from the menu.
&lt;br&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img sr...

&lt;p&gt;Marketers need to know how to track brand visibility in AI search because search has changed. AI-generated answers now show up earlier in the discovery journey, often before a traditional search result. In 2025, nearly a third of Gen Z consumers (31%) defaulted to chatbots or AI to find information online, according to &lt;a href="https://offers.hubspot.com/ai-marketing"&gt;HubSpot’s 2025 AI Trends for Marketers report&lt;/a&gt;, and it’s clear that AI search is growing — rapidly. In 2026, Google reported that AI Mode has over one billion monthly users, and queries have doubled every quarter since launch.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Failure to place brands in &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-overviews"&gt;AI Overviews&lt;/a&gt; (AIO) or within AI search tools like ChatGPT, Perplexity, or Claude is a failure to meet prospective buyers where they’re searching. That’s harmful to audience connection, conversion, and ultimately, revenue. Brands that show up in AI search are the ones that audiences consider. The ones that don’t simply disappear from the conversation, with no ranking position, no impression count, and no obvious way to tell.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This article breaks down what brand visibility is, how to track brand visibility in AI search, the Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) that marketers need to pay attention to, and the best tools to support AI visibility tracking.&lt;/p&gt; 
