&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What is AI search and brand visibility?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI search refers to search that takes place on AI platforms, like ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google Gemini, Microsoft Copilot, and Google’s AI Overviews. There are many more, and AI search platforms are growing. Unlike Google or other search engines, which return a list of links to click through, &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-search-engines"&gt;AI search engines&lt;/a&gt; answer questions directly. Those answers are lifted or paraphrased from trusted sources.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Brand visibility in AI search relates to the impressions that brands get within AI platforms. A brand mentioned or cited in AI in an answer has visibility. Brand visibility in AI search includes mentions, citations, sentiment, and accuracy. The goal is for brands to increase their visibility. They want to be cited and mentioned often, accurately, and favorably.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;How to Track Brand Visibility in AI Search Right Now&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The most basic way to check AI brand visibility is manually: Open ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Copilot. Ask the same set of questions across each one. Then, record whether the brand appears, how it’s described, and whether competitors show up instead. This is an easy starting point. But, it breaks down quickly.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A single spot check only captures one moment in time. That result reflects one user’s search history and personalization, not any broader trend. When multiplied across several platforms and hundreds to thousands of prompt variations, manual tracking becomes a task that nobody has time to maintain.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;HubSpot &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ran-ai-search-experiments"&gt;ran an experiment testing hundreds of queries&lt;/a&gt; across AI chatbots, search engines, and social platforms. The experiment found that AI tools were noticeably weaker at handling specific, niche information, like details about a particular local business, than they were at answering broad or general questions. This is why prompts need to be tracked consistently — and ideally automatically. No one can infer how a platform or brand performs across the board just because it performs well in one prompt, particularly if that prompt is broad and general.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This is where dedicated tools come in.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Rather than replaying the same prompts by hand, tools built for AI brand tracking run them automatically, on a schedule, across multiple engines at once, then organize the results into a usable format, such as:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Which prompts triggered a mention.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Which sources got cited.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;How sentiment trended over time.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Different tools approach brand visibility measurement in different ways; some focus narrowly on mention frequency, others build out fuller scoring models across multiple dimensions.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-grader/"&gt;HubSpot’s free AI Search Grader&lt;/a&gt; is one example. The grader gives marketers a baseline read on AI visibility, and what it provides for free is pretty amazing. Here’s what the start of the report looks like.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://blog.hubspot.com/hs-fs/hubfs/Frank%20H/how-to-track-brand-visibility-in-ai-search-2-20260925-8838088.webp?width=450&amp;amp;height=506&amp;amp;name=how-to-track-brand-visibility-in-ai-search-2-20260925-8838088.webp" width="450" height="506" alt="Screenshot from HubSpot’s brand visibility tool, AI Search Grader, showing the key metrics and how they’re tracked across core AI platforms." style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 450px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;While tools like AI Search Grader are excellent, there are limitations.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI Search Grader captures a baseline read; it doesn’t track improvements or declines in trends. Ideally, most marketing teams will move from a one-time snapshot to ongoing tracking, so that they can adapt marketing strategy based on data.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Tools like &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo"&gt;Hub...

&lt;p&gt;I’ve been experimenting with Claude AI for marketing workflows over the past year, and it’s now my go-to assistant. In this field, AI use is close to universal: 98% of marketing teams use it in some way, according to &lt;a href="https://offers.hubspot.com/ai-marketing"&gt;HubSpot’s State of AI in Marketing report&lt;/a&gt;. For campaign marketers, that might look like turning a product announcement into a week of social posts, tightening ad copy to fit character limits, or pressure-testing campaign messaging before a stakeholder review.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=64493837-f4dd-43b0-abee-3ef154e06092&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Connect HubSpot to Claude For Personalized Insights [Free Connector]" height="58" width="645" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/64493837-f4dd-43b0-abee-3ef154e06092.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;But Claude only knows what you tell it. It can’t automatically see which contacts converted, which campaigns generated pipeline, or what customers keep asking your support team, so even sharp output can miss what’s actually happening in your business.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/claude"&gt;HubSpot connector for Claude&lt;/a&gt; brings your HubSpot CRM data into the conversation and lets Claude act on it, like drafting a marketing email or creating a follow-up task directly in your account. This guide covers how to set up the connector, the marketing workflows and prompts it supports, how to govern its use, and when a dashboard is the better choice.&lt;/p&gt; 
